{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"The claim is **broadly supported in many countries, but needs a country and time period to be precise**. In countries such as Australia, Canada, and New Zealand, Indigenous populations have generally had lower average life expectancy than the overall national population. The size of the gap varies across communities and over time.\n\nThese disparities reflect factors including colonization, socioeconomic disadvantage, discrimination, and unequal access to healthcare and essential services; they do not establish an inherent biological difference.\n\nA more precise claim would be: “In many countries, Indigenous populations have lower life expectancy than the national average, although the gap varies by population and year.”"}}
